# Generic point and function-field pull-backs for elliptic curves

Definition: `EllipticCurve_FunctionFieldPullback` (`cc616e54-6b99-5922-988e-22ce404946c4`), by
Claude, 2026-09-05.

Throughout, $W$ is a Weierstrass curve over a field $R$ and $K$ an $R$-algebra which is a field; $E
= W_{/K}$ denotes the base-changed affine curve, $K[E]$ its Mathlib coordinate ring
$K[X][Y]/(\,W\text{-polynomial})$ and $L = K(E)$ its function field (the fraction field of $K[E]$).
A first group of results concerns $K[E]$ as a $K[X]$-algebra: it is finite and integral over $K[X]$,
every nonzero prime ideal of it is maximal, and for $K$ algebraically closed every nonzero prime
equals $\mathrm{XYIdeal}(a, C\,b) = (X-a,\;Y-b)$ for some solution $(a,b)$ of the affine equation,
these ideals being maximal. `Point.xc`, `Point.yc` read off the coordinates of an affine point
(value $0$ at the point at infinity). The generic point is the $L$-point $\gamma$ whose coordinates
`genericX`, `genericY` are the images in $L$ of the classes of $X$ and $Y$; that it satisfies the
equation, hence is nonsingular when $W$ is elliptic, is proved here. For a nonzero point $Q \in
E(L)$, `pointHom Q` is the $K$-algebra map $K[E] \to L$ determined by $X \mapsto x(Q)$, $Y \mapsto
y(Q)$; it is injective whenever $K$ is algebraically closed and $Q$ is not the base change of a
$K$-point, and `pointPull Q` is then its extension $L \to L$ along the fraction field. Specialising
$Q$ gives `transPull`/`transEquiv` (pull-back along translation by a $K$-point $S$, i.e. the map
sending $\gamma$ to $\gamma + S$), `negPull`/`negEquiv` (pull-back along inversion), and `mulPull W
K n`, defined from the point $n\cdot\gamma$ when the predicate `MulGood W K n` holds — namely
$n\cdot\gamma \ne 0$ and $n\cdot\gamma$ is not the base change of any $K$-point — and as the
identity otherwise. The expected identities are proved: additivity and injectivity of $S \mapsto
\tau_S^{*}$, $\iota^{*}\circ\iota^{*} = \mathrm{id}$, $[m]^{*}[n]^{*} = [nm]^{*}$, and
$\tau_S^{*}\circ[n]^{*} = [n]^{*}$ when $nS = 0$. `placeOf W K P` packages $(X-x(P),\,Y-y(P))$ as a
height-one prime of $K[E]$; it is injective, and surjective onto the height-one spectrum for $K$
algebraically closed and $W$ elliptic, with `CoordinateRing.evalAt` the evaluation homomorphism
$K[E] \to K$ at a solution of the equation. Finally, [`Valuation.Cong v a
b`](../def/EllipticCurve_FunctionFieldPullback.html#L856) is the relation $v(a-b) < 1$ — congruence
modulo the valuation ideal, not an identity — shown stable under sums, products and powers of
elements of valuation $\le 1$, under inversion and division by elements of valuation $1$, and under
two-variable polynomial evaluation; from it and a valuation-transfer lemma for a ring map between
fraction fields of Dedekind domains, the module deduces that $v_P(\mathrm{pointPull}\,Q\,(h)) \le
e^{-k}$ whenever $v_{P'}(h) \le e^{-k}$, for points whose coordinates are congruent at $P$, and the
corresponding statement for `mulPull` in terms of polynomials $\Phi, \Psi, \omega, \psi$ expressing
the coordinates of $n\cdot\gamma$ and of $n\cdot P$ as rational functions of the coordinates.

**Relation to Mathlib.** Mathlib supplies the affine Weierstrass curve, its coordinate ring with
`XClass`, `YClass`, `XYIdeal`, the function field, the group of affine points and base change of
points; the generic point, the homomorphisms `pointHom`/`pointPull` and their translation, inversion
and multiplication-by-$n$ specialisations, the map from points to the height-one spectrum, and the
valuation congruence relation
[`Valuation.Cong`](../def/EllipticCurve_FunctionFieldPullback.html#L856) are the project's own.

**Where it is used.** The material sets up the action of translations, inversion and multiplication
by $n$ on the function field of an elliptic curve, together with the dictionary between affine
points over an algebraically closed field and height-one places of the coordinate ring, and a
valuation comparison between a point $P$ and its multiple $n\cdot P$.

